WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.045
This is the executive
briefing for Book 01 of the

2
00:00:03.045 --> 00:00:06.638
Federal Modernization Workbook,
The Transformation Arc.

3
00:00:06.638 --> 00:00:11.020
It runs about thirty minutes, and it's
for the people who sponsor, fund,

4
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:16.497
secure and authorize the move from a
domain-joined client and server estate to cloud

5
00:00:16.497 --> 00:00:20.879
identity, cloud device management
and governed hybrid operations:

6
00:00:20.879 --> 00:00:24.713
executive sponsors, CIOs, CISOs
and authorizing officials.

7
00:00:24.713 --> 00:00:27.524
The book's argument fits
in three sentences.

8
00:00:27.524 --> 00:00:32.760
For twenty years Active Directory quietly
did governance that nobody wrote down,

9
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.687
so modernization is a governance
rebuild, not a platform swap.

10
00:00:36.687 --> 00:00:39.989
The estate has to be analyzed
before anything moves,

11
00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:44.288
and the work delivered in gated
phases with a rollback at every step.

12
00:00:44.288 --> 00:00:48.850
And leadership's job is to conduct the
orchestra, not to play the instruments.

13
00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:50.687
I'll take four questions in order.

14
00:00:50.687 --> 00:00:52.650
Why modernization is so hard.

15
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.862
What changes across
the platform, identity,

16
00:00:55.862 --> 00:00:59.163
policy, network services,
compute and access.

17
00:00:59.163 --> 00:01:00.875
Who leads the transformation.

18
00:01:00.875 --> 00:01:05.245
And how to analyze the estate, turn
the analysis into delivered change,

19
00:01:05.245 --> 00:01:09.313
and follow a gated roadmap to the
retirement of Active Directory.

20
00:01:09.313 --> 00:01:11.799
There's no configuration on these slides.

21
00:01:11.799 --> 00:01:16.790
Each decision slide names the decision,
who owns it, and when it's needed.

22
00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.540
Start with how the estate got here.

23
00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:24.928
Federal IT reached its current shape in
four eras, and each solved a real problem.

24
00:01:24.928 --> 00:01:29.762
The mainframe held all the data
and also enforced access to it:

25
00:01:29.762 --> 00:01:31.278
one truth, in one box.

26
00:01:31.278 --> 00:01:35.601
Distributed systems put a database
and a directory in every region,

27
00:01:35.601 --> 00:01:38.689
and nobody replaced that
single source of truth.

28
00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:43.456
From 2010, OMB set a target of closing
at least eight hundred data centers,

29
00:01:43.456 --> 00:01:46.792
and agencies stacked
inspection devices between

30
00:01:46.792 --> 00:01:49.890
field offices and the
systems they depended on.

31
00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:54.880
Then email moved to Microsoft 365
and workloads spread across clouds,

32
00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.978
while identity stayed in regional domains.

33
00:01:57.978 --> 00:02:00.934
Now the same person can
appear as several records,

34
00:02:00.934 --> 00:02:03.602
and no one can say which
one is authoritative.

35
00:02:03.602 --> 00:02:05.602
That's why modernization stalls.

36
00:02:05.602 --> 00:02:08.502
Each step assumes the one
before it is finished.

37
00:02:08.502 --> 00:02:11.778
Zero Trust assumes
authoritative cloud identity.

38
00:02:11.778 --> 00:02:14.528
Cloud identity assumes a clean directory.

39
00:02:14.528 --> 00:02:17.353
A clean directory assumes governed groups.

40
00:02:17.353 --> 00:02:19.836
And governed groups
assume someone owns them,

41
00:02:19.836 --> 00:02:21.927
which is where many agencies are stuck.

42
00:02:21.927 --> 00:02:30.296
Meanwhile the mandates arrive with dates:
M-22-09's Zero Trust goals for fiscal

43
00:02:30.296 --> 00:02:34.759
2024, BOD 25-01's baselines by June 20,

44
00:02:34.759 --> 00:02:38.553
2025, and M-26-14's
logging reset in May 2026.

45
00:02:38.553 --> 00:02:41.577
Each assumes a foundation
the estate never built.

46
00:02:41.577 --> 00:02:45.216
So the first decision is about
ownership, not technology.

47
00:02:45.216 --> 00:02:48.627
Before anything is planned,
name the executive sponsor,

48
00:02:48.627 --> 00:02:52.928
the steward who runs the program
office, and the authorizing official.

49
00:02:52.928 --> 00:02:56.670
The roadmap makes that the entry
condition for its first phase.

50
00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.282
Chapter 02 states the problem precisely.

51
00:03:00.282 --> 00:03:04.945
Active Directory shipped with Windows
2000 as an identity directory.

52
00:03:04.945 --> 00:03:09.426
Over twenty-five years every Windows-era
service wired itself into it,

53
00:03:09.426 --> 00:03:14.757
because it was the only enterprise-wide,
governed, replicated namespace available.

54
00:03:14.757 --> 00:03:17.295
Nobody wrote down how much
governance it carried.

55
00:03:17.295 --> 00:03:21.570
The organizational unit (OU)
tree wasn't a folder system.

56
00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:25.848
Placing one user in a branch handed
the account to that branch's delegated

57
00:03:25.848 --> 00:03:29.705
administrators, applied every
Group Policy linked along the path,

58
00:03:29.705 --> 00:03:32.370
fed the tools that built
groups from position,

59
00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:36.508
bound the account to the branch's
delegation, and drew an audit boundary.

60
00:03:36.508 --> 00:03:38.395
One act, five effects.

61
00:03:38.395 --> 00:03:43.807
Entra ID has no OU tree, so each
effect has to be rebuilt on purpose.

62
00:03:43.807 --> 00:03:48.795
Most migration plans count users,
computers, groups and Group Policy.

63
00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:52.352
The program's inventory
counts eleven categories,

64
00:03:52.352 --> 00:03:57.011
adding service accounts and service
principal names, DNS and DHCP,

65
00:03:57.011 --> 00:04:02.770
certificate services, RADIUS, LDAP-bound
applications and trust relationships.

66
00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:06.121
Those fail silently: a
certificate that doesn't renew,

67
00:04:06.121 --> 00:04:10.082
an application whose bind account
was deleted with the domain.

68
00:04:10.082 --> 00:04:12.558
No log line says governance has failed.

69
00:04:12.558 --> 00:04:15.496
Microsoft hasn't deprecated
Active Directory.

70
00:04:15.496 --> 00:04:17.539
It still supports and enhances it,

71
00:04:17.539 --> 00:04:21.845
while pointing customers toward a
cloud-first model reached in phases.

72
00:04:21.845 --> 00:04:26.183
So there's no vendor deadline;
retiring it is the agency's decision.

73
00:04:26.183 --> 00:04:29.659
That makes scope a decision
for the sponsor and the CIO:

74
00:04:29.659 --> 00:04:34.294
fund this as a governance transformation
across all eleven categories,

75
00:04:34.294 --> 00:04:38.520
not a users-and-groups project,
when scope and funding are set.

76
00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:40.470
What has to be true?

77
00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:45.282
The workbook reads federal Zero Trust
guidance as requiring five things.

78
00:04:45.282 --> 00:04:54.144
No single document publishes this list;
it's the program's synthesis of SP 800-207,

79
00:04:54.144 --> 00:04:58.032
M-22-09, CISA's maturity
model and M-26-14.

80
00:04:58.032 --> 00:05:01.410
One: a single authoritative
source of identity,

81
00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.182
one record per user, device and account.

82
00:05:04.182 --> 00:05:06.006
Two: a governed directory,

83
00:05:06.006 --> 00:05:10.695
rationalized before the move to a
flat cloud directory, not after.

84
00:05:10.695 --> 00:05:14.620
Three: continuous verification,
not one-time sign-in.

85
00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:16.952
Four: telemetry used for management,

86
00:05:16.952 --> 00:05:20.295
feeding governance and not
only incident response.

87
00:05:20.295 --> 00:05:23.230
And five: governance
that runs automatically,

88
00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:26.782
because manual review can't
keep up with a hybrid estate.

89
00:05:26.782 --> 00:05:30.320
The third is where the old
world and the new differ most.

90
00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:33.660
In Active Directory a user
signs in once and gets a

91
00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.245
ticket that lasts ten hours,
renewable for seven days.

92
00:05:37.245 --> 00:05:40.422
Entra ID checks tokens
against Conditional Access:

93
00:05:40.422 --> 00:05:42.933
an access token lasts one hour by default,

94
00:05:42.933 --> 00:05:47.735
or up to twenty-eight hours under
continuous access evaluation, which revokes

95
00:05:47.735 --> 00:05:52.020
it within about fifteen minutes of
an event such as a disabled account.

96
00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:54.183
Distance matters, but narrowly.

97
00:05:54.183 --> 00:05:57.922
What it hurts is the operations
that need a domain controller:

98
00:05:57.922 --> 00:06:02.833
hybrid-joined devices need line of sight
for sign-in, password changes and policy.

99
00:06:02.833 --> 00:06:06.466
Light in fiber from Portland,
Oregon to Washington takes about

100
00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:10.520
forty-five milliseconds round trip,
and bandwidth doesn't change that.

101
00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.860
The durable answer is to
remove the dependency.

102
00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.526
Now what changes, starting
with the platform:

103
00:06:17.526 --> 00:06:22.097
one place where every decision is
written down, signed and replayable.

104
00:06:22.097 --> 00:06:27.028
The book's reference design runs
every analysis, plan, delivery and

105
00:06:27.028 --> 00:06:32.497
evidence step on one Windows Server
2025 host, with a standby for recovery.

106
00:06:32.497 --> 00:06:36.161
The property that matters is
a single authoritative writer:

107
00:06:36.161 --> 00:06:37.773
two writers would diverge.

108
00:06:37.773 --> 00:06:41.072
Every change is a signed
commit, never a portal click.

109
00:06:41.072 --> 00:06:44.434
It's built from standard
components; it isn't a product.

110
00:06:44.434 --> 00:06:46.672
Two design rules matter at this level.

111
00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:50.047
First, governance and execution never mix.

112
00:06:50.047 --> 00:06:52.438
The governance layer validates plans,

113
00:06:52.438 --> 00:06:56.397
signs them and writes the audit
trail, but never touches a target.

114
00:06:56.397 --> 00:07:00.509
The execution layer runs authorized
plans and decides nothing.

115
00:07:00.509 --> 00:07:02.716
They run under separate identities,

116
00:07:02.716 --> 00:07:06.835
because a component that both decides
and acts can cover its tracks.

117
00:07:06.835 --> 00:07:09.835
Second, the host is a control-plane asset.

118
00:07:09.835 --> 00:07:13.893
It reads the forest and
writes to cloud identity,

119
00:07:13.893 --> 00:07:19.472
so it's treated as Tier 0: hardened,
and logged centrally under M-26-14.

120
00:07:19.472 --> 00:07:23.672
The program's target posture for
federal production is gated actuation.

121
00:07:23.672 --> 00:07:28.060
The host proposes a correction, and
a person approves it before it runs.

122
00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:32.036
The decision belongs to the
program office, under the steward:

123
00:07:32.036 --> 00:07:36.557
stand up the host as the only
authoritative writer, protected as Tier 0,

124
00:07:36.557 --> 00:07:41.235
and record the standby model and the
actuation stage in the decision log.

125
00:07:41.235 --> 00:07:45.480
Gate G0 needs it operational
by the end of Phase 0.

126
00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:49.542
Identity comes first in
the chain, because policy,

127
00:07:49.542 --> 00:07:52.693
devices and access all
target identity groups.

128
00:07:52.693 --> 00:07:58.355
The OU tree carried delegation, policy
scope and accountability in one structure.

129
00:07:58.355 --> 00:08:02.355
A flat cloud directory has none of
that until the program writes it down.

130
00:08:02.355 --> 00:08:07.258
The book rebuilds it with four Microsoft
mechanisms bound into one model:

131
00:08:07.258 --> 00:08:12.242
attributes, dynamic groups, administrative
units and Conditional Access.

132
00:08:12.242 --> 00:08:17.609
Setting a user's attributes from the HR
record produces every downstream effect:

133
00:08:17.609 --> 00:08:23.217
group membership, delegated scope, policy
targeting, licenses and drift visibility.

134
00:08:23.217 --> 00:08:25.280
One stamp, five outcomes.

135
00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.905
That only works if the
attributes are true.

136
00:08:27.905 --> 00:08:33.392
M-22-09 requires centralized identity
management for agency users.

137
00:08:33.392 --> 00:08:37.917
For the workforce, the authoritative
source is the HR system of record.

138
00:08:37.917 --> 00:08:42.317
HR owns who works for the agency,
where they sit and who they report to.

139
00:08:42.317 --> 00:08:47.268
A dynamic rule is only as trustworthy as
whoever can write the attribute it reads,

140
00:08:47.268 --> 00:08:50.741
so the help-desk and self-service
write paths are removed,

141
00:08:50.741 --> 00:08:53.105
and HR provisioning is the only writer.

142
00:08:53.105 --> 00:08:56.317
Then joiner, mover and
leaver run on their own.

143
00:08:56.317 --> 00:09:01.118
Tokens don't fix a wrong model; they
sign broken authorizations efficiently.

144
00:09:01.118 --> 00:09:04.434
So HR decides what the
authoritative attributes mean,

145
00:09:04.434 --> 00:09:07.381
and identity engineering
decides how they flow.

146
00:09:07.381 --> 00:09:10.405
Contractors need an authoritative
source of their own.

147
00:09:10.405 --> 00:09:15.160
Gate G1 requires the HR feed
working before any rollout.

148
00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:17.273
Policy is next.

149
00:09:17.273 --> 00:09:22.208
Group Policy was Active Directory's most
ambitious delivery system, and its most

150
00:09:22.208 --> 00:09:26.848
network-bound: a client applies it only
when it can reach a domain controller.

151
00:09:26.848 --> 00:09:30.998
A cloud-native endpoint gets its
configuration from the cloud instead.

152
00:09:30.998 --> 00:09:34.741
The replacement is several
surfaces: Intune for devices,

153
00:09:34.741 --> 00:09:39.601
Conditional Access for access control,
and Azure machine configuration,

154
00:09:39.601 --> 00:09:44.222
through Arc, for servers, because
Intune doesn't manage Windows Server.

155
00:09:44.222 --> 00:09:47.248
The program's pattern is
regenerate, not copy.

156
00:09:47.248 --> 00:09:50.075
Porting a fifty-setting
policy as fifty modern

157
00:09:50.075 --> 00:09:53.484
settings carries forward dead
weight and old conflicts.

158
00:09:53.484 --> 00:09:57.573
Microsoft's own recommendation is
to start new rather than migrate.

159
00:09:57.573 --> 00:09:59.847
Four rules govern every change.

160
00:09:59.847 --> 00:10:02.747
Each setting has exactly one owning layer.

161
00:10:02.747 --> 00:10:04.650
Require is preferred to block,

162
00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:09.290
because a bad block locks users out and
a bad require adds a step you can fix;

163
00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:12.866
blocks are kept for cases such
as legacy authentication,

164
00:10:12.866 --> 00:10:15.148
which the federal baseline demands.

165
00:10:15.148 --> 00:10:19.072
Every change passes plan
validation, a simulated tenant,

166
00:10:19.072 --> 00:10:22.161
a report-only pilot, and
only then production.

167
00:10:22.161 --> 00:10:26.735
And the emergency access accounts are
excluded from every blocking policy.

168
00:10:26.735 --> 00:10:32.062
The decision is the CISO's: the control
requirements every surface must meet,

169
00:10:32.062 --> 00:10:36.723
meaning the Conditional Access
baseline, MFA strength and logging.

170
00:10:36.723 --> 00:10:41.011
Gate G1 needs that baseline
enforced, with no user outside

171
00:10:41.011 --> 00:10:46.110
the MFA requirement except the
documented emergency accounts.

172
00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:51.458
Network services are the part
organizations most often defer,

173
00:10:51.458 --> 00:10:55.260
because DNS, DHCP and address
management still work.

174
00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:58.398
That's exactly how they keep
the old directory alive.

175
00:10:58.398 --> 00:11:00.336
The three form one surface.

176
00:11:00.336 --> 00:11:04.497
DNS usually lives in zones
integrated with Active Directory,

177
00:11:04.497 --> 00:11:08.577
so every domain controller sits
in the name-resolution path,

178
00:11:08.577 --> 00:11:11.923
and Active Directory can't
retire until DNS moves.

179
00:11:11.923 --> 00:11:16.511
DHCP servers are authorized in the
forest, but that check is Windows

180
00:11:16.511 --> 00:11:21.673
policing itself: it stops an unauthorized
Windows server, not a rogue device.

181
00:11:21.673 --> 00:11:24.693
And address management,
in many organizations,

182
00:11:24.693 --> 00:11:27.172
is a spreadsheet one person maintains.

183
00:11:27.172 --> 00:11:30.472
Without it, DNS and DHCP drift.

184
00:11:30.472 --> 00:11:32.548
So the program moves them together.

185
00:11:32.548 --> 00:11:37.117
One governed IPAM authority
covers the whole address surface,

186
00:11:37.117 --> 00:11:41.772
on-premises and cloud, and DNS and
DHCP derive their records from it.

187
00:11:41.772 --> 00:11:46.672
Disagreement between them is drift,
and there's no DNS-only run.

188
00:11:46.672 --> 00:11:52.991
Federal controls point the same
way: SP 800-53 treats secure name

189
00:11:52.991 --> 00:11:56.959
resolution and the component
inventory as controls of their own.

190
00:11:56.959 --> 00:12:00.577
The common failure is to
modernize identity and policy

191
00:12:00.577 --> 00:12:03.723
and leave these for a Phase
2 that rarely happens.

192
00:12:03.723 --> 00:12:07.684
A forest that still hosts
DNS has not been retired.

193
00:12:07.684 --> 00:12:12.734
That makes it a scope and funding
decision for the sponsor and the CIO:

194
00:12:12.734 --> 00:12:18.048
put DNS, DHCP and IPAM in the core
program now, with one IPAM authority.

195
00:12:18.048 --> 00:12:21.310
The pilot division's
zones move in Phase 2.

196
00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:22.847
Compute.

197
00:12:22.847 --> 00:12:26.194
Once identity, policy and
network services have moved,

198
00:12:26.194 --> 00:12:30.061
domain-joined machines are the
largest remaining dependency.

199
00:12:30.061 --> 00:12:34.915
A machine leaves only when its identity,
its policy and its trust have each

200
00:12:34.915 --> 00:12:39.372
been replaced, so the domain join is
the last thing to go, not the first.

201
00:12:39.372 --> 00:12:42.707
User PCs end Entra joined
and managed in Intune,

202
00:12:42.707 --> 00:12:46.298
and every hardware refresh
should arrive that way.

203
00:12:46.298 --> 00:12:50.749
Hybrid join is a transition: there's
no supported way to convert a

204
00:12:50.749 --> 00:12:55.536
hybrid-joined PC without resetting
it, so time that reset to a refresh.

205
00:12:55.536 --> 00:12:59.085
A program that leaves devices
hybrid joined hasn't finished.

206
00:12:59.085 --> 00:13:00.735
Servers take a different road.

207
00:13:00.735 --> 00:13:05.092
Azure Arc projects them into Azure
for policy and posture while they stay

208
00:13:05.092 --> 00:13:09.735
where they are, and leaving the domain is
a separate decision for each workload.

209
00:13:09.735 --> 00:13:12.610
What holds servers in the
domain is applications.

210
00:13:12.610 --> 00:13:16.515
Every Kerberos service principal
name is an application,

211
00:13:16.515 --> 00:13:21.803
and each gets one of three answers: migrate
to modern authentication, retire it,

212
00:13:21.803 --> 00:13:27.010
or, as a last resort, keep it on a small
documented island with an end-of-life date.

213
00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:30.085
NTLM is resolved in the same pass.

214
00:13:30.085 --> 00:13:34.848
And an application's compute stays with
its data until its round trips are reduced,

215
00:13:34.848 --> 00:13:37.510
because distance multiplies
with every call.

216
00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:41.822
Only an application's owner can make
that call and sign off its cutover.

217
00:13:41.822 --> 00:13:47.501
Keeping legacy authentication open is
a risk acceptance, so the authorizing

218
00:13:47.501 --> 00:13:53.800
official sets every island and NTLM end
date, and every name is resolved by Gate G4.

219
00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:55.812
The access plane.

220
00:13:55.812 --> 00:14:00.937
When Active Directory retires, being
inside the network stops meaning anything.

221
00:14:00.937 --> 00:14:05.927
M-22-09 says users should log in to
applications rather than networks,

222
00:14:05.927 --> 00:14:09.669
every application should be
treated as internet-accessible,

223
00:14:09.669 --> 00:14:14.438
and multifactor authentication belongs
at the application layer, not in a VPN.

224
00:14:14.438 --> 00:14:18.236
The replacement has four
surfaces: Zero Trust as the model,

225
00:14:18.236 --> 00:14:22.983
Conditional Access deciding each
request, a secure access service edge

226
00:14:22.983 --> 00:14:27.651
replacing the VPN, and phishing-resistant
authentication as the proof.

227
00:14:27.651 --> 00:14:34.570
M-22-09 requires it for staff, contractors
and partners, and BOD 25-01 makes

228
00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:40.562
blocking legacy authentication, and
phishing-resistant MFA for all users, mandatory.

229
00:14:40.562 --> 00:14:45.662
FIDO2 keys, passkeys, Windows
Hello, and PIV or CAC cards through

230
00:14:45.662 --> 00:14:50.574
certificate-based authentication
qualify; SMS and voice don't.

231
00:14:50.574 --> 00:14:53.100
Two consequences need an executive eye.

232
00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:57.288
First, smart-card sign-in is
anchored in Active Directory today.

233
00:14:57.288 --> 00:15:01.213
The card and PIN stay, and
validation moves to Entra ID.

234
00:15:01.213 --> 00:15:03.607
But issuance is a separate decision:

235
00:15:03.607 --> 00:15:07.199
an enterprise certificate
authority depends on AD DS,

236
00:15:07.199 --> 00:15:11.270
and Microsoft's cloud PKI covers
only Intune-managed devices,

237
00:15:11.270 --> 00:15:15.500
so issuance needs a successor
before Active Directory retires.

238
00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:20.621
Second, privilege: every human admin
assignment becomes eligible and time-bound,

239
00:15:20.621 --> 00:15:23.220
standing credentials go through a vault,

240
00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:26.812
and emergency accounts are
tested at least every 90 days.

241
00:15:26.812 --> 00:15:31.927
The CISO decides: phishing-resistant
MFA as the end state for everyone,

242
00:15:31.927 --> 00:15:37.995
with FIDO2 or certificate-based authentication
for Tier 0 administrators first,

243
00:15:37.995 --> 00:15:43.300
opening in Phase 1, and certificate
issuance moved off AD DS by Gate G4.

244
00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:44.900
Who leads?

245
00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:46.838
The book's framing is simple.

246
00:15:46.838 --> 00:15:51.175
Microsoft provides the instruments;
the program provides the orchestra.

247
00:15:51.175 --> 00:15:54.552
Cloud identity, device
management, Conditional Access,

248
00:15:54.552 --> 00:15:59.325
privileged identity management and the
assessment tools are each excellent.

249
00:15:59.325 --> 00:16:01.375
But look at what each one doesn't decide.

250
00:16:01.375 --> 00:16:05.800
Cloud identity doesn't decide what your
organizational structure should become.

251
00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:09.150
Hybrid management doesn't
decide which servers retire.

252
00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:12.575
The assessment tools don't decide
what to do with the findings.

253
00:16:12.575 --> 00:16:16.287
Every entry in that column is
a decision someone has to own.

254
00:16:16.287 --> 00:16:21.214
And no vendor deadline forces it, so
without leadership organizations migrate in

255
00:16:21.214 --> 00:16:25.850
pieces and finish with a surface that
can't answer last year's audit questions.

256
00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:29.908
And the program isn't a product:
it needs no license of its own,

257
00:16:29.908 --> 00:16:33.718
but the services it orchestrates
do, from Entra ID P1 for

258
00:16:33.718 --> 00:16:38.438
Conditional Access to Entra ID
Governance for lifecycle workflows.

259
00:16:38.438 --> 00:16:42.826
Federal policy already says this
belongs to more than the IT shop.

260
00:16:42.826 --> 00:16:48.665
M-22-09 says the whole C-suite must be
aligned and committed, and it required

261
00:16:48.665 --> 00:16:53.612
each agency to designate a zero trust
implementation lead within 30 days.

262
00:16:53.612 --> 00:16:59.841
M-19-17 requires an agency-wide identity
governance structure that includes HR,

263
00:16:59.841 --> 00:17:02.600
general counsel, privacy and acquisition.

264
00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:06.835
So the first Who decision, for the
sponsor and the CIO before Phase 0:

265
00:17:06.835 --> 00:17:09.300
confirm who already holds those roles,

266
00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:13.510
and decide whether one of them
becomes the program's steward.

267
00:17:14.260 --> 00:17:18.460
The book's decision matrix runs
on one rule: one decider per row.

268
00:17:18.460 --> 00:17:21.866
The single deliberate
exception is the pilot gate,

269
00:17:21.866 --> 00:17:24.785
G2, which the steward
and the sponsor co-sign.

270
00:17:24.785 --> 00:17:29.963
The sponsor and the CIO own scope, funding
and sequence, break ties, and decide

271
00:17:29.963 --> 00:17:35.635
when Active Directory components retire,
because each retirement ends a fallback.

272
00:17:35.635 --> 00:17:38.816
They clear gate G3 with
the steering committee,

273
00:17:38.816 --> 00:17:42.160
and G5, the retirement of
Active Directory itself.

274
00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:46.822
Their job is to keep it a program, not
five projects that finish one at a time.

275
00:17:46.822 --> 00:17:51.297
The CISO decides the control
requirements every surface must meet.

276
00:17:51.297 --> 00:17:56.340
The authorizing official is the only
official who can accept risk on the agency's

277
00:17:56.340 --> 00:18:01.533
behalf, so every place the program carries
risk instead of removing it reaches that

278
00:18:01.533 --> 00:18:04.469
desk: a deferred
legacy-authentication cutoff,

279
00:18:04.469 --> 00:18:08.985
a Kerberos island with no end date, a
deviation from a mandatory baseline.

280
00:18:08.985 --> 00:18:12.835
The program office, led by the
steward, runs the orchestra.

281
00:18:12.835 --> 00:18:17.334
It holds the repository, turns
assessments into authorized plans

282
00:18:17.334 --> 00:18:21.260
and evaluates the gates, but it
doesn't decide risk or scope.

283
00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:24.497
The platform teams do the
work inside each domain.

284
00:18:24.497 --> 00:18:27.947
Application owners decide
for their own applications.

285
00:18:27.947 --> 00:18:30.647
HR decides what the
worker attributes mean.

286
00:18:30.647 --> 00:18:35.665
The sponsor's decision, before the program
starts: fill in the matrix with names,

287
00:18:35.665 --> 00:18:38.443
one decider per row,
and get the authorizing

288
00:18:38.443 --> 00:18:42.040
official's agreement to
every risk-acceptance row.

289
00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:45.715
Three decisions often
land on the wrong desk,

290
00:18:45.715 --> 00:18:48.165
and the book moves each one on purpose.

291
00:18:48.165 --> 00:18:52.428
First, end dates for legacy
authentication and NTLM.

292
00:18:52.428 --> 00:18:57.514
They sit with the authorizing official, not
with the engineers who'd like more time,

293
00:18:57.514 --> 00:19:01.627
because keeping legacy authentication
open is a risk acceptance,

294
00:19:01.627 --> 00:19:05.666
and BOD 25-01 makes blocking
it a mandatory baseline.

295
00:19:05.666 --> 00:19:08.376
Authorizing officials may accept risk for

296
00:19:08.376 --> 00:19:11.690
deviations from those
policies, under agency policy.

297
00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:15.215
Second, the help desk's
identity-verification procedure.

298
00:19:15.215 --> 00:19:19.728
The help desk supports users through
every wave, and that makes it a target.

299
00:19:19.728 --> 00:19:24.114
CISA's advisory on the Scattered
Spider group describes attackers

300
00:19:24.114 --> 00:19:28.577
phoning help desks to get passwords
reset and MFA tokens transferred.

301
00:19:28.577 --> 00:19:33.452
So the procedure for resets and temporary
access passes is a CISO decision.

302
00:19:33.452 --> 00:19:36.740
The help desk carries it out
with narrowly scoped roles.

303
00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:38.327
Third, records.

304
00:19:38.327 --> 00:19:43.627
Retiring Active Directory retires the
systems that hold years of access records.

305
00:19:43.627 --> 00:19:48.944
NARA's General Records Schedule 3.2 keeps
some system access records for six years

306
00:19:48.944 --> 00:19:53.401
after the account is terminated, and
once a domain controller is gone,

307
00:19:53.401 --> 00:19:56.841
its logs can't be recovered
for an audit or a legal hold.

308
00:19:56.841 --> 00:20:01.848
So the records officer decides retention
before anything is decommissioned:

309
00:20:01.848 --> 00:20:06.240
any domain controller, federation
farm, sync server or SIEM connector.

310
00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:08.958
And the privacy office is consulted before

311
00:20:08.958 --> 00:20:12.180
any new attribute flow
carries personal data.

312
00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:15.618
Now how to deliver it,
starting with the estate.

313
00:20:15.618 --> 00:20:19.140
You can't modernize what you haven't
enumerated, and nothing can be

314
00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:23.055
retired until someone has written
down everything it was quietly doing.

315
00:20:23.055 --> 00:20:27.881
Ask what's in a long-lived forest and the
honest answer is usually more than anyone

316
00:20:27.881 --> 00:20:30.978
on the current team knows:
trusts no one can fully map,

317
00:20:30.978 --> 00:20:33.643
Group Policy whose
authors left a decade ago,

318
00:20:33.643 --> 00:20:38.252
certificate templates whose permissions
open privilege-escalation paths,

319
00:20:38.252 --> 00:20:42.430
and servers past support, each of
which needs a disposition in the plan.

320
00:20:42.430 --> 00:20:44.680
The assessment follows three rules.

321
00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:49.167
It's read-only, enforced in the
account, the code and the audit trail.

322
00:20:49.167 --> 00:20:53.907
It's least-privilege: never a domain
or enterprise admin, starting from

323
00:20:53.907 --> 00:20:59.218
default read rights and adding only
narrow, recorded additions, never secrets.

324
00:20:59.218 --> 00:21:03.111
And every run carries
provenance: who ran it, when,

325
00:21:03.111 --> 00:21:07.492
against what, with which rights,
and a hash of every file.

326
00:21:07.492 --> 00:21:09.742
The engine rejects a run without it.

327
00:21:09.742 --> 00:21:13.174
The output maps privileged
groups and service accounts,

328
00:21:13.174 --> 00:21:16.167
so it's stored encrypted
and handled as sensitive.

329
00:21:16.167 --> 00:21:19.796
And because broad enumeration
looks like reconnaissance,

330
00:21:19.796 --> 00:21:21.943
security operations is told first.

331
00:21:21.943 --> 00:21:25.595
One more input decides whether
an application may move:

332
00:21:25.595 --> 00:21:29.804
how many round trips it makes to
its data, measured, not estimated.

333
00:21:29.804 --> 00:21:32.267
The steward commissions it in Phase 0.

334
00:21:32.267 --> 00:21:36.447
In the program's experience a first
pass takes days to a few weeks,

335
00:21:36.447 --> 00:21:38.790
and Gate G0 needs it committed.

336
00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:43.115
Then the cycle that turns
analysis into delivered change.

337
00:21:43.115 --> 00:21:45.540
Nothing touches the target without a plan,

338
00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:49.490
and no plan touches it without someone
of record having read it first.

339
00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:52.532
Analyze compares the current
state with the target

340
00:21:52.532 --> 00:21:55.078
and produces a diff and
a blast-radius score.

341
00:21:55.078 --> 00:22:00.239
Plan turns the diff into a sequence of
actions, each bounded to a single object,

342
00:22:00.239 --> 00:22:03.868
ordered by dependency and
paired with its own inverse,

343
00:22:03.868 --> 00:22:05.803
so the plan can run backwards.

344
00:22:05.803 --> 00:22:07.027
Then two gates.

345
00:22:07.027 --> 00:22:10.283
Validation rejects any
plan that breaks naming,

346
00:22:10.283 --> 00:22:14.515
scope or safety rules, such as
deleting too many objects at once.

347
00:22:14.515 --> 00:22:17.084
Authorization needs a review and at least

348
00:22:17.084 --> 00:22:19.653
one approver of record
who isn't the author.

349
00:22:19.653 --> 00:22:23.986
Only then does the execution
layer, under a separate identity,

350
00:22:23.986 --> 00:22:27.640
deliver each action, verify
it and record the result.

351
00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:32.452
When something fails, the default is
to halt, and rollback is explicit.

352
00:22:32.452 --> 00:22:35.418
Every run ends in a
signed evidence packet,

353
00:22:35.418 --> 00:22:39.739
and when nothing has drifted, the
next run is a zero-action plan.

354
00:22:39.739 --> 00:22:44.545
A separate rule caps how far
automation may go: record, observe,

355
00:22:44.545 --> 00:22:49.915
advise, gated actuation, and autonomy
only for named low-risk classes.

356
00:22:49.915 --> 00:22:53.555
Tenant-wide role changes,
Conditional Access enforcement

357
00:22:53.555 --> 00:22:56.527
and bulk device changes
stay gated permanently.

358
00:22:56.527 --> 00:22:59.052
No federal document requires that ceiling.

359
00:22:59.052 --> 00:23:03.427
The book chooses it because it's a
posture an authorizing official can sign.

360
00:23:03.427 --> 00:23:05.966
So before the first production delivery,

361
00:23:05.966 --> 00:23:10.295
the authorizing official agrees the
ceiling for each class of change,

362
00:23:10.295 --> 00:23:14.090
with its dry run, its approver
and its rollback on record.

363
00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:19.652
The roadmap sequences all of this as an
illustrative fifty-two-week program.

364
00:23:19.652 --> 00:23:22.790
The weeks are a sequencing
model, not a commitment;

365
00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:25.852
large federal estates
typically run multi-year.

366
00:23:25.852 --> 00:23:28.189
There are seven phases and six gates.

367
00:23:28.189 --> 00:23:32.022
Phase 0, pre-flight, stands
up the platform host

368
00:23:32.022 --> 00:23:35.552
and runs the assessment;
G0 is Ready to Plan.

369
00:23:35.552 --> 00:23:39.841
Phase 1, foundation, puts the
identity primitives in place,

370
00:23:39.841 --> 00:23:45.202
including the access baseline and the
HR feed; G1 is Foundation Complete.

371
00:23:45.202 --> 00:23:49.346
Phase 2 proves the whole
pattern on one pilot division,

372
00:23:49.346 --> 00:23:54.041
typically IT; G2, Pilot Clean,
needs zero drift for a hold period.

373
00:23:54.041 --> 00:23:59.740
Phase 3, the longest, rolls out division
by division, two or three at a time.

374
00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:03.641
Phase 4 retires legacy
policy, groups, DNS,

375
00:24:03.641 --> 00:24:09.377
DHCP and the last service principal
names; G4 is Legacy Quiet.

376
00:24:09.377 --> 00:24:13.414
Phase 5, the shortest, retires AD DS.

377
00:24:13.414 --> 00:24:15.640
And Phase 6 is steady state.

378
00:24:15.640 --> 00:24:17.277
Two properties define it.

379
00:24:17.277 --> 00:24:22.167
The five workstreams, identity,
policy, services, compute and access,

380
00:24:22.167 --> 00:24:24.874
run in parallel, each with a named lead;

381
00:24:24.874 --> 00:24:29.327
finishing one before starting
the next doubles the duration.

382
00:24:29.327 --> 00:24:33.627
And it's gated: a phase opens only
when the previous gate clears.

383
00:24:33.627 --> 00:24:36.516
The decision closes the loop on ownership.

384
00:24:36.516 --> 00:24:39.070
Put the gate dates on
the sponsor's calendar,

385
00:24:39.070 --> 00:24:42.903
and the end date of every exception
on the authorizing official's.

386
00:24:42.903 --> 00:24:46.410
Gate G0 gets its date in Phase 0.

387
00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:49.260
Two things make the gates trustworthy.

388
00:24:49.260 --> 00:24:52.484
First, every gate criterion
is evaluated from the

389
00:24:52.484 --> 00:24:56.023
repository and the drift
reports, not from a signature.

390
00:24:56.023 --> 00:25:01.122
Zero drift for fourteen days is a
query; so is every PC cloud-joined.

391
00:25:01.122 --> 00:25:04.034
The engine reports green,
the steward concurs,

392
00:25:04.034 --> 00:25:07.635
and the record that merges the
decision shows who made it.

393
00:25:07.635 --> 00:25:11.060
A gate the engine can't evaluate
needs a better criterion.

394
00:25:11.060 --> 00:25:13.895
Second, rollback triggers are thresholds,

395
00:25:13.895 --> 00:25:17.622
not judgment calls, and the
values are the agency's to set.

396
00:25:17.622 --> 00:25:21.247
Drift above the threshold for
more than a day halts the phase.

397
00:25:21.247 --> 00:25:24.447
Conditional Access blocking
more than a set share

398
00:25:24.447 --> 00:25:27.723
of legitimate sign-ins means
the policy is reverted.

399
00:25:27.723 --> 00:25:31.547
If drift detection stops
reporting, all phase work halts.

400
00:25:31.547 --> 00:25:33.711
Authority is fixed in advance.

401
00:25:33.711 --> 00:25:37.635
The steward clears G0, G1 and G4.

402
00:25:37.635 --> 00:25:40.447
The steward and the sponsor co-sign G2.

403
00:25:40.447 --> 00:25:43.747
The sponsor and the steering
committee clear G3.

404
00:25:43.747 --> 00:25:48.761
And the sponsor and the CIO clear
G5, Active Directory retired.

405
00:25:48.761 --> 00:25:50.347
And be clear about the end.

406
00:25:50.347 --> 00:25:53.654
A forest in backup isn't
a lasting fallback:

407
00:25:53.654 --> 00:25:59.322
a backup is valid only within the
tombstone lifetime, 180 days by default.

408
00:25:59.322 --> 00:26:03.810
The real rollback point is the last
moment a domain controller still runs.

409
00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:07.811
So G5 is the sponsor's and the
CIO's decision, made on evidence:

410
00:26:07.811 --> 00:26:12.466
no live production dependency on
Active Directory authentication,

411
00:26:12.466 --> 00:26:16.590
or a documented Kerberos island
with an end-of-life date.

412
00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:21.315
Two external clocks bear on the
schedule, and they're easy to conflate.

413
00:26:21.315 --> 00:26:26.384
FedRAMP's Consolidated Rules
for 2026, announced on June 25,

414
00:26:26.384 --> 00:26:30.315
2026, govern how cloud
services are certified.

415
00:26:30.315 --> 00:26:34.715
They become mandatory for
everyone on January 1, 2027,

416
00:26:34.715 --> 00:26:41.515
and FedRAMP stops accepting new Rev5
certification applications on June 11, 2027.

417
00:26:41.515 --> 00:26:44.415
None of that certifies
the agency's own estate.

418
00:26:44.415 --> 00:26:49.569
FedRAMP is explicit that each agency still
authorizes its own federal information

419
00:26:49.569 --> 00:26:54.503
systems, and should never create a standalone
authorization for a cloud service.

420
00:26:54.503 --> 00:27:00.052
What the 2027 date changes is the evidence
your providers publish and you inherit.

421
00:27:00.052 --> 00:27:05.670
The deadlines that bind the agency
directly come from OMB and CISA:

422
00:27:05.670 --> 00:27:09.080
M-26-14's logging plan
and maturity levels,

423
00:27:09.080 --> 00:27:17.003
and BOD 25-01's secure configuration
baselines for in-scope Microsoft 365 tenants.

424
00:27:17.003 --> 00:27:19.052
The estate has its own dates too.

425
00:27:19.052 --> 00:27:24.003
Windows 10 reached end of
support on October 14, 2025.

426
00:27:24.003 --> 00:27:31.936
Extended security updates for Windows Server
2012 and 2012 R2 ended on October 13,

427
00:27:31.936 --> 00:27:38.703
2026, and Windows Server 2016 reaches
end of support on January 12, 2027.

428
00:27:38.703 --> 00:27:41.002
Mainframes have a place in the sequence.

429
00:27:41.002 --> 00:27:44.285
Token-based access joins
the access workstream late,

430
00:27:44.285 --> 00:27:49.730
discrete read-mostly transactions first,
and M-22-09 notes that mainframes

431
00:27:49.730 --> 00:27:54.578
may need other zero trust mechanisms
where endpoint detection tools don't fit.

432
00:27:54.578 --> 00:27:59.516
So the sponsor and the CIO sequence
the program against the agency's own

433
00:27:59.516 --> 00:28:05.400
deadlines and end-of-support dates, starting
now, not against FedRAMP's 2027 date.

434
00:28:06.150 --> 00:28:08.850
So, the decisions, with their owners.

435
00:28:08.850 --> 00:28:13.009
Agency leadership and the CIO name
the sponsor, the steward and the

436
00:28:13.009 --> 00:28:18.000
authorizing official, and the sponsor
fills the matrix with one decider per row.

437
00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:23.467
The sponsor and the CIO scope all eleven
categories, with DNS, DHCP and address

438
00:28:23.467 --> 00:28:29.275
management in the core program, and at the
end retire Active Directory on evidence.

439
00:28:29.275 --> 00:28:34.190
The steward stands up the platform
host as the one authoritative writer,

440
00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:39.350
protected as Tier 0, and commissions the
read-only assessment, both in Phase 0.

441
00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:43.512
The CISO sets the control
requirements, enforced at Gate G1.

442
00:28:43.512 --> 00:28:46.276
HR defines what the
worker attributes mean.

443
00:28:46.276 --> 00:28:49.331
The authorizing official
agrees the actuation

444
00:28:49.331 --> 00:28:51.775
ceiling and every exception's end date.

445
00:28:51.775 --> 00:28:56.325
And the records officer settles retention
before anything is decommissioned.

446
00:28:56.325 --> 00:28:59.412
None of this requires a
vendor decision on day one.

447
00:28:59.412 --> 00:29:04.435
It requires an inventory, an owner
and a date: the read-only assessment,

448
00:29:04.435 --> 00:29:08.400
a named sponsor and steward,
and Gate G0 on the calendar.

449
00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:10.875
Microsoft has supplied the instruments.

450
00:29:10.875 --> 00:29:12.495
The program is the orchestra,

451
00:29:12.495 --> 00:29:16.275
and the people named in this briefing
are the ones who have to play it.

452
00:29:16.275 --> 00:29:20.016
Retiring Active Directory is
